The Security Council,

Guided by the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations,

Recalling its relevant resolutions on the situation in the Middle East, including the Palestinian question,

Expressing grave concern over the ongoing escalation of violence and the rapid deterioration of the situation, resulting in heavy civilian casualties and catastrophic humanitarian suffering in the Gaza Strip,

Stressing that the protection of civilians is paramount and that all parties must comply with their obligations under international law, including international humanitarian law and international human rights law,

Emphasizing that civilians in Israel and the occupied Palestinian territory, including East Jerusalem, must be protected in accordance with international humanitarian law,

Gravely concerned by the dire humanitarian situation in the Gaza Strip, including the acute shortages of water, electricity, fuel, food, and medical supplies, and underlining the urgent need for safe, rapid, unhindered, and sustainable humanitarian access,

Reaffirming that a just and lasting solution to the Israeli-Palestinian conflict can only be achieved through peaceful means, based on relevant United Nations resolutions and the two-State solution,

1. Calls for an immediate, durable and fully respected humanitarian ceasefire;

2. Strongly condemns all violence and hostilities against civilians, and all acts of terrorism;

3. Condemns the heinous attacks by Hamas on 7 October 2023 and the taking of civilian hostages;

4. Firmly condemns indiscriminate attacks against civilians as well as against civilian objects and infrastructure in the Gaza Strip, resulting in massive civilian casualties, in particular strikes affecting medical and humanitarian facilities;

5. Demands the immediate, safe, and unconditional release of all civilian hostages, ensuring their safety, well-being, and humane treatment in compliance with international law;

6. Calls for the immediate rescission of the order for civilians and United Nations personnel to evacuate all areas in Gaza north of Wadi Gaza and relocate to the south;

7. Urges the continuous, sufficient, and unhindered provision of essential goods and services to civilians, including water, electricity, fuel, food, and medical supplies, as well as the unhindered access of humanitarian personnel and aid throughout the Gaza Strip;

8. Emphasizes the importance of preventing any regional spillover of the conflict, and calls upon all relevant actors to exercise maximum restraint;

9. Reaffirms its unwavering commitment to the two-State solution, with two democratic States, Israel and Palestine, living side by side in peace within secure and recognized borders, on the basis of the 1967 lines;

10. Decides to remain seized of the matter.

